### What is the Jump Game problem?

Jump Game gives you an array of non-negative integers where each value represents the maximum number of positions you can jump forward from that index. Starting at index zero, you must determine whether you can reach the last index. This is a reachability problem that can be solved optimally with a greedy approach.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class JumpGame {
    public boolean canJump(int[] nums) {
        int farthest = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (i > farthest) return false;
            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
            if (farthest >= nums.length - 1) return true;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

### Why does the greedy approach work?

The greedy works because reachability is monotone. If you can reach index `i`, you can reach every index from zero to `i`. This means the entire history of how you got to any index collapses into a single number: the farthest index reachable so far. You never need to remember which exact path was taken, only how far you can currently get.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MonotoneReachability {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {2, 3, 1, 1, 4};
        int farthest = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
            System.out.println("Index " + i + ": value=" + nums[i]
                + " farthest=" + farthest);
        }
        // Index 0: value=2 farthest=2
        // Index 1: value=3 farthest=4  (reaches last index)
        // Index 2: value=1 farthest=4
        // Index 3: value=1 farthest=4
        // Index 4: value=4 farthest=8
    }
}
```

```text
Index 0: value=2 farthest=2
Index 1: value=3 farthest=4
Index 2: value=1 farthest=4
Index 3: value=1 farthest=4
Index 4: value=4 farthest=8
```

### How does the stuck condition work?

At each step, before extending the reach, we check whether the current index is even reachable. If `i > farthest`, it means no earlier index could have jumped to position `i`, so we are stranded. This check is the only way the algorithm returns false.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class StuckDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {3, 2, 1, 0, 4};
        int farthest = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (i > farthest) {
                System.out.println("Stuck at index " + i
                    + ": farthest was " + farthest);
                return; // cannot reach last index
            }
            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
            System.out.println("Index " + i + ": farthest=" + farthest);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Index 0: farthest=3
Index 1: farthest=3
Index 2: farthest=3
Index 3: farthest=3
Stuck at index 4: farthest was 3
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The algorithm performs a single left-to-right scan of the array, doing constant work at each index. It uses a single integer variable to track the farthest reach.

- **Time complexity** – O(n) for one pass through the array.
- **Space complexity** – O(1) because only one integer variable is used.
- **Comparison** – the equivalent DP approach uses a boolean array where `dp[i]` marks whether index `i` is reachable, costing O(n) space and O(n squared) time in the worst case.

```java
// Greedy: O(n) time, O(1) space
int farthest = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (i > farthest) return false;
    farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
}

// DP alternative: O(n^2) time, O(n) space
boolean[] dp = new boolean[n];
dp[0] = true;
for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
        if (dp[j] && j + nums[j] >= i) { dp[i] = true; break; }
```

### How is this different from Jump Game II (minimum jumps)?

Jump Game asks only whether the end is reachable (boolean answer), while Jump Game II asks for the minimum number of jumps to reach the end (integer answer). Jump Game II uses a BFS-like greedy that tracks the current jump boundary and counts layers.

| Aspect | Jump Game (I) | Jump Game II |
|---|---|---|
| Question | Can you reach the end? | Minimum jumps to reach end? |
| Answer type | Boolean | Integer |
| Strategy | Single farthest-reach variable | BFS-like layer counting |
| Variables | `farthest` | `farthest`, `currentEnd`, `jumps` |
| Complexity | O(n) time, O(1) space | O(n) time, O(1) space |

```java
// Jump Game II: minimum jumps
public int jump(int[] nums) {
    int jumps = 0, currentEnd = 0, farthest = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {
        farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
        if (i == currentEnd) {
            jumps++;
            currentEnd = farthest;
        }
    }
    return jumps;
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single-element array (already at the end, return true), an array starting with zero (stuck at the start unless it is the only element), and an array where every element is zero except the first.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class JumpEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        JumpGame jg = new JumpGame();

        // Single element: already at end
        System.out.println(jg.canJump(new int[]{0}));         // true

        // Starts with zero, length > 1: stuck
        System.out.println(jg.canJump(new int[]{0, 1}));      // false

        // All zeros except first
        System.out.println(jg.canJump(new int[]{4, 0, 0, 0, 0})); // true

        // Large jump at start
        System.out.println(jg.canJump(new int[]{100, 0, 0})); // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
true
true
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most frequent mistake is forgetting the stuck check before updating the reach. Without it, a zero-value index that should block progress gets silently passed. Another mistake is not handling the early termination when farthest already covers the last index.

```java
// WRONG: no stuck check
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]); // updates even if unreachable!
}
return farthest >= n - 1; // may report true incorrectly

// CORRECT: check before updating
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (i > farthest) return false;              // stuck check first
    farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
    if (farthest >= n - 1) return true;          // early exit
}
```

### How would you apply this in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, a chained fund transfer passes through intermediary accounts where each account can forward the funds a limited number of legs ahead based on its clearing limit. Before initiating the chain, the system checks whether the funds can reach the final settlement account.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class SettlementChainValidator {
    public boolean canReachSettlement(List<Integer> clearingLimits) {
        int farthest = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < clearingLimits.size(); i++) {
            if (i > farthest) return false;
            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + clearingLimits.get(i));
            if (farthest >= clearingLimits.size() - 1) return true;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should recognise that the greedy collapses an O(n squared) DP into O(n) by exploiting the monotone reachability property. They should also know that the greedy frontier is essentially a BFS frontier of size one, and that this pattern extends to other reachability problems. The key insight for interviews is: when reachability only grows, a single frontier variable captures the entire state.

```java
// Senior insight: the greedy frontier is equivalent to
// maintaining a set of reachable indices, but because
// reachability is monotone (contiguous from 0), the set
// collapses to a single integer: the farthest index.
//
// This is why the greedy is correct and the DP is redundant.
```
